WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.935
In this video, we explore how we investigate
the root cause of a test problem with the

00:00:04.935 --> 00:00:06.140
MachinePulseAI Agent.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.436
We follow the complete process in the
application, from selecting a model and

00:00:11.436 --> 00:00:15.680
sending a request to running analyses and
preparing the final report.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.140
Let's explore the application's capabilities
through an aircraft example.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:25.600
In this example, an engineer notices
increasing gearbox vibration during cruise.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.420
The same symptom persists in a ground check.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:33.260
We investigate the increase using the flight
and ground-test recordings.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:39.720
First, we select the Qwen3 Coder 30B model
running on our computer and connect to it.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.500
In this example, the Agent uses a local model
to conduct the analysis.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.440
We also add the documents used in the
investigation to the Knowledge Base.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:52.160
These contain technical information such as
sensor roles and bearing geometry.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.351
The documents are stored locally and can be
reused for relevant questions in later

00:00:58.351 --> 00:00:58.980
sessions.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.055
We describe the observed increase in vibration
and include the flight and ground-test

00:01:04.055 --> 00:01:05.460
recordings in our request.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:11.240
We ask the Agent to investigate the cause of
the increase and report its findings.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.460
Then we send the request.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:19.608
The Agent opens the recordings and uses RAG to
retrieve the sensor roles and bearing geometry

00:01:19.608 --> 00:01:21.060
from the Knowledge Base.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.480
After checking channel data quality, it
configures and runs the analysis tools.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.840
We follow its work through the assistant's
messages and the analysis windows.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:37.900
It first compares speed, torque and vibration
in the early and late sections of the flight.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:43.040
The average speed remains unchanged while the
mean vibration indicator rises.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:48.765
FFT and Order Tracking analyses examine the
vibration components alongside rotational

00:01:48.765 --> 00:01:49.140
speed.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.340
The Agent compares the finding in the flight
recording with the ground test.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.525
It identifies an outer-race frequency family
near 107.4 Hz in both sensors, in both the

00:01:59.525 --> 00:02:01.500
flight and ground recordings.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.320
The finding persists in the test after the
sensor is remounted.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.680
This weakens an explanation based solely on
one sensor's mounting.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.128
The engineer checks the Agent's initial
interpretations against the measurements and

00:02:14.128 --> 00:02:15.640
makes the necessary corrections.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.160
The final assessment directs the investigation
toward the bearing.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.668
Confirming this explanation requires a
physical inspection of the bearing and its

00:02:23.668 --> 00:02:24.120
mounting,

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.700
followed by another test under comparable
conditions.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.640
When the investigation is complete, we open
the final report.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:37.580
The report brings together the analyses, plots
and reference documents used by the Agent.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.260
The engineer can review the evidence and plan
the next physical inspection.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.200
This example uses synthetic data.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.400
To see the same process with your own test
recording, request a demo on our website.
